<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>CFBundleDevelopmentRegion</key>
    <string>$(DEVELOPMENT_LANGUAGE)</string>
    <key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
    <string>$(EXECUTABLE_NAME)</string>
    <key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
    <string>$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)</string>
    <key>CFBundleInfoDictionaryVersion</key>
    <string>6.0</string>
    <key>CFBundleName</key>
    <string>$(PRODUCT_NAME)</string>
    <!--
      The Home-screen name is translated in `Resources/*.lproj/InfoPlist.strings`, and unlike the
      driver app's it is **transliterated rather than translated** into Sinhala and Tamil. C076 made
      the same call and argued it: this is the *consumer* app, its icon sits on a home screen beside
      apps labelled in Sinhala, and AL-26's whole point is that a Sinhala-first passenger is not
      handed an English product. The driver app needed no such decision because its name carries a
      translatable noun.
    -->
    <key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
    <string>MageRide</string>
    <key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
    <string>APPL</string>
    <key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
    <string>$(MARKETING_VERSION)</string>
    <key>CFBundleVersion</key>
    <string>$(CURRENT_PROJECT_VERSION)</string>

    <!--
      The three languages CLAUDE.md fixes. Listed so iOS negotiates against exactly this set: a
      handset set to Hindi resolves to `en` (the development region) rather than to whichever
      `.lproj` happened to sort first.
    -->
    <key>CFBundleLocalizations</key>
    <array>
        <string>si</string>
        <string>ta</string>
        <string>en</string>
    </array>

    <!-- Portrait only. Every frame in `specs/wireframes/passenger_ios.html` is a portrait handset. -->
    <key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations</key>
    <array>
        <string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
    </array>

    <key>UILaunchScreen</key>
    <dict>
        <!-- §0.2's `primary`, resolved from the asset catalogue so the launch surface is the
             brand's rather than the system's white. -->
        <key>UIColorName</key>
        <string>primary</string>
    </dict>

    <!--
      **One background mode, and the absence of the other is the point.** `remote-notification` is
      P-02's silent `location_request` data message and US-6A.15's scheduled reminder — the two
      pushes this app renders itself. `location` is deliberately NOT declared: this app publishes
      nothing (D3' §3.3 makes position ingest the driver's MQTT plane), the location source has
      exactly three consumers and all three are on screen, and a passenger app that kept the GPS
      running with the screen off would be spending a battery to recompute the same nineteen cells.
      `apps/passenger-android`'s `ManifestTest` asserts the same absence from the other side.
    -->
    <key>UIBackgroundModes</key>
    <array>
        <string>remote-notification</string>
    </array>

    <!--
      Purpose strings. English here so the key exists at all; the translations iOS actually shows
      are in `Resources/*.lproj/InfoPlist.strings`. A missing key is a hard crash the first time the
      API is called, which is why they are declared even though every value is overridden.

      **When-in-use only.** The Android twin declares no `ACCESS_BACKGROUND_LOCATION` either;
      SCR-PA-005's rationale screen asks for exactly this grant and nothing in this app runs a fix
      with the screen off.

      `NSCameraUsageDescription` arrived with **C098**, which is the commit that opens a camera:
      SCR-PI-017's driver-QR scan (AL-22, AL-47) is the only use, and the app renders no QR of its
      own. The fence held until then, exactly as `apps/driver-ios` kept its own out until C087 needed
      the document scanner — a purpose string with no API behind it asks for a permission the app
      does not want, and App Review reads the list as a statement of intent.
    -->
    <key>NSLocationWhenInUseUsageDescription</key>
    <string>Your location centres the live map, shows the vehicles within about 3 km of you, and fills in your pickup point.</string>
    <key>NSCameraUsageDescription</key>
    <string>The camera reads your driver's payment QR code so you can pay the fare from your own bank app.</string>

    <!--
      Every build-time value the app reads, in one dictionary, interpolated from the xcconfig.
      `PassengerApp/DI/PassengerEnvironment.swift` is the only file allowed to read it — a build flag
      read at two call sites is a flag that gets overridden at one of them.

      **No MQTT keys.** This app has no broker host and never will (D3' §3.3); the SignalR hub rides
      `ApiBaseUrl`, so there is one origin rather than two.
    -->
    <key>MageRide</key>
    <dict>
        <key>ApiBaseUrl</key>
        <string>$(MAGERIDE_API_BASE_URL)</string>
        <key>PMTilesUrl</key>
        <string>$(MAGERIDE_PMTILES_URL)</string>
    </dict>

    <!--
      ATS. No exception is declared, so every request is HTTPS — including the Debug configuration's
      `http://localhost`, which ATS exempts for loopback without an entry. An
      `NSAllowsArbitraryLoads` here would be a debug convenience that shipped.
    -->
    <key>NSAppTransportSecurity</key>
    <dict>
        <key>NSAllowsLocalNetworking</key>
        <true/>
    </dict>
</dict>
</plist>
